1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:11,368
In the early hours of the tenth of October, 1996, armed rebels broke into a girls' boarding school at Aboke, in northern Uganda.

2
00:00:12,468 --> 00:00:19,273
They tied the pupils together and marched them into the bush. One hundred and thirty-nine girls were taken.

3
00:00:20,373 --> 00:00:32,000
The school's deputy head, an Italian nun named Sister Rachele Fassera, followed them on foot. She persuaded the rebel commander to release a hundred and nine. He kept thirty.

4
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:40,844
Those girls were among tens of thousands of children abducted by the Lord's Resistance Army over two decades.

5
00:00:41,944 --> 00:00:47,235
The war it fought drove most of the population of northern Uganda into camps.

6
00:00:48,335 --> 00:00:58,404
Its leader, Joseph Kony, is one of the most wanted men in the world. As of 2025, he has never been arrested. This is his story.

7
00:01:00,604 --> 00:01:12,081
Joseph Kony was born around 1961 in Odek, a village near the town of Gulu. He is Acholi, from the main ethnic group of the region.

8
00:01:13,181 --> 00:01:23,464
He served as an altar boy as a child, and dropped out of school. As a young man he became known locally as a healer who claimed to be possessed by spirits.

9
00:01:24,564 --> 00:01:36,041
In 1986, a rebel army led by Yoweri Museveni seized power in Uganda. As of 2025, he is still the country's president.

10
00:01:37,141 --> 00:01:48,128
Many soldiers of the defeated government were Acholi, and they fled north. Museveni's troops followed them, and abuses against civilians bred fear and resentment.

11
00:01:49,228 --> 00:02:00,663
A spirit medium named Alice Lakwena raised a rebel force, the Holy Spirit Movement, and marched on the capital. It was crushed in 1987.

12
00:02:01,763 --> 00:02:11,405
Kony gathered his own band of fighters, which became the Lord's Resistance Army. He said he would rule Uganda according to the Ten Commandments.

13
00:02:13,605 --> 00:02:21,605
The LRA never won wide support. Short of recruits, it turned on the very people it claimed to fight for: the Acholi.

14
00:02:22,705 --> 00:02:31,516
Kony ran the LRA like a cult. He claimed that spirits spoke through him, and his fighters were bound by strict rules and rituals.

15
00:02:32,616 --> 00:02:46,120
Its main weapon was abduction. Fighters raided villages and schools at night and took children. Boys were made to fight and carry loads. Girls were forced to become the so-called wives of commanders.

16
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:56,116
New recruits were often forced to take part in killings, sometimes in their own villages, so that they would feel they could never go home.

17
00:02:57,216 --> 00:03:11,851
Civilians suspected of helping the government were killed or maimed. At Atiak in 1995, and at Barlonyo in 2004, LRA fighters killed hundreds of people in a single day.

18
00:03:12,951 --> 00:03:25,623
In the 1990s, the government of Sudan armed and sheltered the LRA, in retaliation for Uganda's support for rebels in southern Sudan. The war became a proxy war.

19
00:03:26,723 --> 00:03:38,648
In 1994, a government minister, Betty Bigombe, met Kony in the bush and came close to a peace deal. The talks collapsed, and the war went on for more than a decade.

20
00:03:40,848 --> 00:03:52,411
The Ugandan government's answer was to move civilians into camps, often by force. By 2005, nearly two million people in northern Uganda lived in them.

21
00:03:53,511 --> 00:04:07,975
Conditions were desperate. A 2005 survey by Uganda's health ministry and the World Health Organization estimated about a thousand excess deaths a week, mostly from disease and malnutrition.

22
00:04:09,075 --> 00:04:22,024
Every evening, thousands of children walked into the towns to sleep on verandas, in bus stations and in church compounds, where they were safer from abduction. They became known as night commuters.

23
00:04:23,124 --> 00:04:38,911
Human rights groups also documented killings, rapes and beatings by Ugandan government soldiers. For years, the outside world paid little attention. A senior UN official called it one of the world's most neglected crises.

24
00:04:41,111 --> 00:04:55,895
In 2003, Uganda's government referred the situation to the new International Criminal Court. In 2005, the court issued its first-ever arrest warrants: for Kony and four of his commanders.

25
00:04:56,995 --> 00:05:11,864
Kony was charged with crimes against humanity and war crimes, including murder, enslavement, sexual slavery and forcing children to fight. He has never been tried, and these remain charges, not convictions.

26
00:05:12,964 --> 00:05:21,305
In 2006, peace talks began in Juba, in southern Sudan, with mediators from the region and the UN.

27
00:05:22,405 --> 00:05:29,189
Kony met journalists in the jungle that year. He denied that his fighters had committed atrocities.

28
00:05:30,289 --> 00:05:42,599
A ceasefire brought calm to northern Uganda. But Kony never came out to sign the final agreement. Even so, the fighting in Uganda was over, and people slowly began leaving the camps.

29
00:05:44,799 --> 00:06:00,756
By then the LRA had moved into the forests where Congo, South Sudan and the Central African Republic meet. In December 2008, a regional offensive, backed by the United States, attacked its camps. Kony escaped.

30
00:06:01,856 --> 00:06:12,224
His fighters took revenge on villagers. Human Rights Watch documented more than eight hundred civilians killed around Christmas 2008 alone.

31
00:06:13,324 --> 00:06:23,031
A year later, around Makombo in Congo, LRA fighters killed more than three hundred people in four days, according to Human Rights Watch.

32
00:06:24,131 --> 00:06:36,952
In March 2012, an American charity, Invisible Children, released a film called Kony 2012. It was watched about a hundred million times in under a week.

33
00:06:38,052 --> 00:06:47,396
Critics said the film oversimplified the conflict, and arrived years after the LRA had left Uganda. But it pushed governments to act.

34
00:06:48,496 --> 00:07:03,259
The United States had sent about a hundred special forces advisers to help hunt him, and later offered up to five million dollars for information. The mission ended in 2017 without finding him.

35
00:07:05,459 --> 00:07:18,835
One of the men charged alongside Kony shows the LRA's double tragedy. Dominic Ongwen was abducted as a boy of about ten, on his way to school. He rose to become a senior commander.

36
00:07:19,935 --> 00:07:33,332
He surrendered in 2015. In 2021, the court convicted him of sixty-one crimes, including murder and sexual slavery, and sentenced him to twenty-five years.

37
00:07:34,432 --> 00:07:45,504
His defence argued that he was a victim too. The judges accepted that he had suffered as a child, but found him responsible for the crimes he committed as an adult.

38
00:07:46,604 --> 00:08:00,705
Of the others charged in 2005, Raska Lukwiya and Okot Odhiambo were killed. Vincent Otti, Kony's deputy, was reportedly killed on Kony's own orders in 2007.

39
00:08:02,905 --> 00:08:15,727
As of 2025, Kony is still at large. He is believed to be hiding with a small band of followers in the remote borderlands between the Central African Republic and Sudan.

40
00:08:16,827 --> 00:08:29,840
In 2025, ICC judges held a hearing on the charges against him in his absence, the first of its kind at the court. A trial still cannot begin unless he is caught.

41
00:08:30,940 --> 00:08:43,633
In northern Uganda, the abducted children are now adults. Thousands came home under an amnesty, and many still live with what they were forced to do, and with the fear and suspicion of their neighbours.

42
00:08:44,733 --> 00:08:58,493
In 2012, millions of people learned Joseph Kony's name in a few days. More than a decade later, he is still free. Being famous, it turns out, is not the same as being brought to justice.
